/* 科目A — English overlay. Keys: notion number → {s, e, t, ex:[…]} */
window.DATA = window.DATA || {};
window.DATA["FE-grammaire-en"] = {
1:{s:"Converting a number from one base to another",
   e:"The exam mostly asks for binary ↔ decimal ↔ hexadecimal. To go from decimal to binary, divide by 2 until the quotient is zero, then read the remainders from the bottom up. To go from binary to hexadecimal, group the bits in fours from the right: four bits are exactly one hex digit. For the fractional part, multiply by 2 and read the integer parts from the top down.",
   t:"Octal uses 3 bits per digit, hexadecimal 4 — that is the most common mix-up. Also, most decimal fractions never terminate in binary (0.1 is infinite in base 2), which is exactly why floating-point arithmetic rounds.",
   ex:["Read the remainders bottom-up, not top-down.",
       "Split into 4-bit groups from the right; each group gives one hex digit.",
       "For the fractional part, read the integer parts in the order they come out."]},
2:{s:"How computing time grows as the data grows",
   e:"We do not measure seconds but an order of growth: how much more work is needed as the number of items n increases. Linear search looks at everything, so O(n). Binary search halves the range each step, so O(log n). Fast sorts (quicksort, merge sort) average O(n log n); simple sorts (bubble, selection, insertion) are O(n²). Constant factors are dropped: O(2n) is written O(n).",
   t:"Do not confuse average and worst case: quicksort averages O(n log n) but degrades to O(n²) at worst. And binary search requires data that is « already sorted » — on unsorted data it does not apply.",
   ex:["That is the point of the logarithm: a thousand times more data only doubles the work.",
       "The number of comparisons is how many times you can halve the set."]},
3:{s:"Average access time when a cache sits in front of main memory",
   e:"Cache memory is small and fast, main memory large and slow. When the wanted data is already in the cache it is a hit; otherwise main memory must be read. The average time is therefore weighted by the hit rate. This is one of the best-value formulas in the exam: it comes up nearly every session and the calculation is a single line.",
   t:"The question sometimes gives the « miss rate » instead of the hit rate — use 1 − that value. Another trap: some wordings charge the cache time « in addition to » main memory on a miss, so read the statement precisely.",
   ex:["Nine times out of ten you pay 10 ns, one time out of ten you pay 100 ns.",
       "The average collapses as soon as the hit rate drops: keeping it high is the whole point."]},
4:{s:"The share of time during which the system is working",
   e:"MTBF is the mean time between failures, MTTR the mean time to repair. Availability is the fraction of time in service. With two units « in series », one failure stops everything, so the rates multiply. With two units « in parallel », one working unit is enough, so you compute the chance that both fail at once and subtract it from 1.",
   t:"The classic mistake is adding the rates in parallel (0.9 + 0.9 = 1.8, impossible). Remember the direction: series goes down, parallel goes up. If your result moves the other way, the formula is the wrong way round.",
   ex:["The machine runs 900 hours and is repaired in 100: it is available 90 % of the time.",
       "In series, availability is always « lower » than that of a single unit.",
       "In parallel it is always « higher » — that is what redundancy buys."]},
5:{s:"Splitting tables to remove redundancy and anomalies",
   e:"A badly designed table repeats the same fact in several places: change it in one place and not the other, and the data contradicts itself. Normalisation splits it step by step. First normal form: one value per cell, no repeating groups. Second: move out what depends on only « part » of the primary key. Third: move out what depends on a column that is « not » the primary key.",
   t:"Second normal form is only an issue when the primary key spans « several columns ». With a single-column key you go straight from first to third. Also note that normalising multiplies joins, so people sometimes denormalise deliberately for performance.",
   ex:["The product name depends on only half the key, so it moves to a Products table.",
       "The department name depends on an ordinary column, not the key, so it moves to a Departments table."]},
6:{s:"The seven layers that divide up a network communication",
   e:"The model splits communication into seven levels, from the cable to the application. What the exam really wants is « which layer each device and each protocol belongs to »: repeater at layer 1, switch and bridge at layer 2, router and IP at layer 3, TCP and UDP at layer 4. One question in two on this topic comes down to that.",
   t:"Do not confuse the MAC address (layer 2, burned into the hardware) with the IP address (layer 3, assigned by the network). And remember TCP and UDP are both at layer 4: what separates them is reliability, not the layer.",
   ex:["The router reads the IP address: network layer.",
       "The switch reads the MAC address: data link layer."]},
7:{s:"Which key encrypts, which key signs, and what each one guarantees",
   e:"In public-key cryptography everyone holds two linked keys: a public one anyone may have, and a private one never shared. What one key encrypts, only the other can decrypt. To « send a secret », encrypt with the recipient’s public key: only they can read it. To « sign », do the opposite: encrypt with your own private key, and anyone can verify with your public key that it really came from you.",
   t:"Always ask two questions: « whose key », and « public or private ». Encrypting uses the recipient’s key; signing uses the sender’s. Another exam point: public-key cryptography is slow, so in practice it is used only to deliver a symmetric key, and the data itself is encrypted with that.",
   ex:["Only B holds the matching private key, so only B can read it.",
       "If verification succeeds, the document came from A and has not been altered."]},
8:{s:"A fixed-length fingerprint that reveals the slightest change",
   e:"A hash function turns any data into a fingerprint of fixed length. Three properties are tested: you cannot work back to the original (one-way), the tiniest change in the input completely changes the output, and it is extremely hard to find two inputs with the same fingerprint (a collision). It is used to check that a file has not been altered, and to store passwords without ever keeping them in clear text.",
   t:"Hashing is not encryption: encryption is undone with a key, hashing is not undone at all. For passwords, a per-account « salt » is added, otherwise two users with the same password would share the same fingerprint.",
   ex:["The server never knows the password: it compares two fingerprints.",
       "Signing a short digest is far faster than signing the whole document."]},
9:{s:"Proving who you are, and the attacks aimed at that proof",
   e:"Authentication rests on three families of proof: something you know, something you have, something you are. Multifactor authentication combines « different » families. On the attack side, distinguish brute force (every possible try), dictionary attacks (common words), credential stuffing (reusing identifier/password pairs leaked elsewhere) and rainbow tables (precomputed hashes).",
   t:"Do not confuse « two factors » with « two steps »: asking twice for something you know is two steps but one factor. And credential stuffing works because passwords are reused, not because they are weak.",
   ex:["Two different families: that really is multifactor.",
       "Two proofs from the same family add no protection."]},
10:{s:"The malware families and what separates them",
   e:"The exam mostly tests the distinction. A virus needs a host file to spread. A worm reproduces on its own and travels across the network without help. A Trojan horse pretends to be useful software and does not reproduce. Ransomware encrypts the data and demands payment. Spyware and keyloggers quietly steal information.",
   t:"The discriminating question is always the same: does it need a host file, and does it reproduce by itself? Virus = host yes, reproduces yes. Worm = host no, reproduces yes. Trojan = host no, reproduces no.",
   ex:["It is the autonomy of propagation that defines a worm.",
       "It does not duplicate itself: the user installs it."]},
11:{s:"Separating the inside, the outside, and what you expose on purpose",
   e:"A firewall decides which communications pass, according to rules. The DMZ is an intermediate zone holding the servers reachable from the Internet (web, mail): if one is compromised, the attacker is still not inside the internal network. An intrusion detection system reports suspicious traffic; an intrusion prevention system goes further and blocks it. A web application firewall specialises in attacks against web applications.",
   t:"DMZ, VLAN and VPN are often mixed up: the DMZ isolates public servers, the VLAN splits a network logically, the VPN builds an encrypted tunnel across an untrusted network. Three different problems.",
   ex:["A compromised public server gives no access to the internal network.",
       "That is the only difference worth remembering between the two."]},
12:{s:"SQL injection, cross-site scripting, request forgery: the flaw and its countermeasure",
   e:"SQL injection slips SQL code into an input field to hijack the query; the fix is prepared statements with parameters, never string concatenation. Cross-site scripting makes a script run in another user’s browser; the fix is escaping special characters on output. Cross-site request forgery makes a logged-in user perform an action unknowingly; the fix is a unique token per form.",
   t:"Questions often pair the wrong fix with the flaw. Remember: SQL → prepared statement, HTML output → escaping, sensitive action → token. Limiting input length or encrypting the connection fixes none of the three.",
   ex:["The input is interpreted as code instead of being handled as data.",
       "The victim is not the site but the other visitors."]},
13:{s:"Managing risk in advance rather than suffering the incident",
   e:"Security is measured on three axes: confidentiality (only authorised people get in), integrity (the information is not altered), availability (it is there when needed). Faced with a risk there are four answers: reduce it (technical measures), avoid it (drop the activity), transfer it (insurance, outsourcing) or accept it (protection would cost more than the damage).",
   t:"Transferring does not remove the risk, it moves the financial loss. And acceptance is a legitimate decision, not an oversight: you record it when protection would cost more than the incident.",
   ex:["The impact is reduced without removing the threat.",
       "The risk remains, but a third party carries the financial loss."]},
14:{s:"Three ways to find a value, and what each one demands",
   e:"Linear search walks through the elements one by one: it always works, but costs n comparisons at worst. Binary search compares with the middle element and throws away half the range each round: very fast, but it requires data that is already sorted. Hashing computes the position directly from the value: near-instant access, at the price of a larger table and of handling collisions.",
   t:"Binary search is tempting but unusable on unsorted data — and sorting first costs O(n log n), which only pays off if you search often. With hashing, two values can land on the same slot: that is a collision, resolved by chaining or probing.",
   ex:["On average you stop halfway; at worst you walk the whole set.",
       "You halve ten times to land on a single element."]},
15:{s:"Simple sorts and fast sorts, and when each one wins",
   e:"Simple sorts compare elements pairwise: bubble sort (bubble the largest up), selection sort (find the minimum and place it), insertion sort (insert each element into the already sorted part). All are O(n²). Efficient sorts divide the problem: quicksort partitions around a pivot, merge sort splits in two and merges. Both average O(n log n).",
   t:"Do not recite complexities without their conditions. Quicksort is fastest in practice but has a bad worst case; merge sort guarantees O(n log n) but needs extra memory; insertion sort beats everything on small, nearly sorted data.",
   ex:["It is the only simple sort that really benefits from nearly sorted data.",
       "The pivot decides everything: badly chosen, it divides nothing."]},
16:{s:"Two elementary structures that differ by the order things come out",
   e:"A stack always releases the last item put in: you push on top and pop from the top. It is used to remember a way back — function calls, undo, expression evaluation. A queue always releases the first item put in: it handles things in order of arrival — print queues, buffers, scheduling. The exam very often asks you to follow a sequence of operations and say what comes out.",
   t:"Function calls, and therefore recursion, rely on a stack: each call pushes its context. That is exactly why recursion without a stopping condition causes a stack overflow.",
   ex:["Last in, first out.",
       "First in, first out."]},
17:{s:"The notation imposed in 科目B: read it before trying to solve it",
   e:"科目B presents algorithms in a pseudo-language defined by IPA. A procedure is declared with ○, variables with « type: name », assignment uses an arrow ←. Structures are closed explicitly: endif, endwhile, endfor. Arrays are written in braces and indexed in brackets starting at 1; a two-dimensional array is indexed [row, column]. A variable with no value is called 未定義 (undefined).",
   t:"The main trap for someone who already programs: indices start at 1, equality is written = not ==, and there is no integer division as such — mod gives the remainder. Reading the type declarations before tracing the algorithm avoids half the mistakes.",
   ex:["The arrow always points from the value to the variable.",
       "Careful: indexing starts at 1, not at 0 as in most languages."]},
18:{s:"The three basic operations on relational tables",
   e:"Selection keeps certain rows matching a condition: it cuts horizontally. Projection keeps certain columns: it cuts vertically. A join links two tables through a shared column, typically a foreign key pointing at the other table’s primary key. The exam often shows two tables and a result, and asks which combination of operations produces it.",
   t:"Selection and projection are regularly swapped: remember that 選択 cuts rows (horizontal) and 射影 cuts columns (vertical). And a join without a condition produces the Cartesian product — every combination — a common mistake when the linking clause is forgotten.",
   ex:["Rows are kept: the column structure does not change.",
       "Columns are kept: the number of rows does not change (duplicates aside).",
       "The shared column is the seam between the two tables."]},
19:{s:"The order of the clauses, and above all WHERE versus HAVING",
   e:"SELECT picks the columns, FROM the table, WHERE filters rows before any grouping. GROUP BY gathers rows sharing a value so an aggregate (COUNT, SUM, AVG, MAX, MIN) can be applied. HAVING then filters the resulting groups. ORDER BY sorts the final result. That order is not negotiable and almost every session asks about it.",
   t:"The golden rule: an aggregate function cannot appear in WHERE, because when WHERE runs the groups do not exist yet. And every non-aggregated column in the SELECT must appear in the GROUP BY.",
   ex:["The filter applies to individual rows.",
       "The filter applies to groups that have already been computed."]},
20:{s:"The four guarantees that keep a database from contradicting itself",
   e:"A transaction is a set of operations that must all succeed or all fail. Atomicity guarantees all-or-nothing. Consistency guarantees the database is in a valid state before and after. Isolation guarantees concurrent transactions do not see each other half-done. Durability guarantees that once committed, the work survives a crash.",
   t:"Do not confuse consistency and isolation: consistency is about the database’s own rules (constraints), isolation about the effect of concurrent transactions. And remember ROLLBACK undoes everything since the start of the transaction, not just the last statement.",
   ex:["The canonical example: half a transfer would be money lost.",
       "The commit is written durably before it is announced."]},
21:{s:"Stopping two transactions from damaging each other, without freezing both",
   e:"When two transactions touch the same data they must be serialised. A shared lock allows several simultaneous reads but no write. An exclusive lock blocks every other access. Deadlock happens when each transaction holds what the other is waiting for: neither can move. The system detects it and sacrifices one by rolling it back.",
   t:"Locking fixes inconsistency but creates waiting: the wider and longer the locks, the lower the throughput. It is a trade-off, not a free fix. And two simultaneous reads are never a problem — only writing requires exclusivity.",
   ex:["Each waits for what the other holds: the wait is circular and permanent.",
       "That is the simplest cure: impose a common acquisition order."]},
22:{s:"Putting the database back on its feet after a crash, using the log",
   e:"The database logs every change with its before and after value. After a crash, transactions that had not been committed are undone by replaying the before values: that is rollback. Transactions committed but not yet written to disk are replayed from the after values: that is roll-forward. A periodic checkpoint limits how much log has to be re-read.",
   t:"The reflex: check whether the transaction had been committed when the crash happened. Committed → redo it (after values). Not committed → undo it (before values). The classic error is swapping the two logs.",
   ex:["The work was committed: it is reconstructed.",
       "The work was not committed: it is erased."]},
23:{s:"Splitting an address range, and counting the machines it allows",
   e:"An IPv4 address is 32 bits. The mask says where the network part ends: bits set to 1 belong to the network, bits at 0 identify the machine. /24 is shorthand for « 24 network bits ». The number of addressable machines is 2 to the power of the host bits, minus two: the all-zero address names the network itself, the all-one address is the broadcast.",
   t:"Forgetting the « minus two » is the most common slip. And mind the direction: the larger the number after the slash, the smaller the network. /30 leaves only 2 usable addresses, which is what links two routers.",
   ex:["Two addresses are always reserved: the network and the broadcast.",
       "Two bits borrowed from the host part give four subnets."]},
24:{s:"Reliability against speed, on the same floor of the model",
   e:"Both are transport protocols, so layer 4. TCP sets up a connection first, numbers the segments, acknowledges them and retransmits what is missing: delivery is guaranteed and ordered, at the cost of overhead. UDP sends without any connection or acknowledgement: lighter and faster, but packets may be lost or arrive out of order.",
   t:"The trap is assuming UDP sits on another layer because it is « less complete »: both are squarely at layer 4. And TCP opens its connection in three steps (three-way handshake), a detail asked regularly.",
   ex:["An incomplete file is useless: reliability wins.",
       "Retransmitting audio that arrived late is pointless."]},
25:{s:"Which protocol does what, and the port that goes with it",
   e:"Questions almost always target the role and the port. HTTP serves web pages, HTTPS serves them encrypted. SMTP sends mail, POP3 and IMAP receive it — IMAP leaving the messages on the server. DNS turns a domain name into an IP address. DHCP automatically assigns an address to a machine joining the network.",
   t:"Classic confusion between SMTP and POP: SMTP is for sending (S as in Send), POP for receiving. And do not mix up DNS (name → address) with DHCP (handing out addresses): both deal with addresses but solve different problems.",
   ex:["Without it you would have to remember numeric addresses.",
       "The address is lent for a limited time, not given permanently."]},
26:{s:"How long it takes to send a given amount of data over a given line",
   e:"Three steps, always the same. Convert the amount of data into bits (one byte is eight bits). Work out the usable speed by multiplying the line speed by the transmission efficiency. Divide the first by the second. The difficulty is never the formula but the units: megabytes versus megabits, and an efficiency given as a percentage.",
   t:"The near-universal mistake is forgetting to multiply bytes by eight. Sanity check: the answer must be longer than with 100 % efficiency. If it is shorter, the efficiency was applied the wrong way round.",
   ex:["Bits first: 5 × 8 = 40 Mbit.",
       "The advertised speed is never the speed you get: efficiency makes the difference."]},
27:{s:"The order of the phases, and the symmetry between design and testing",
   e:"Development goes down from the need to the code, then back up by testing. Each design level has a matching test level: unit testing checks a module alone, integration testing checks that modules fit together, system testing checks the whole against the specification, acceptance testing is run by the customer to accept delivery. That symmetry is what the V-model describes.",
   t:"Do not confuse system testing with acceptance testing: the first is run by the development team against the specification, the second by the customer against their own needs. And unit testing always comes first, never after integration.",
   ex:["External design: screens and functions. Internal design: modules and data.",
       "The customer pronounces acceptance, not the team that built it."]},
28:{s:"Testing from what the program must do, or from how it is written",
   e:"Black-box testing ignores the code and starts from the specification. Two techniques dominate: equivalence partitioning (one representative per class of values) and boundary value analysis (test just below, on, and just above the limit). White-box testing starts from the code and measures coverage: statement coverage (every line run at least once) or decision coverage (every branch taken both ways).",
   t:"Statement coverage is weaker than decision coverage: you can run 100 % of the lines without ever testing the false side of a condition. And black-box is the only option for acceptance testing, since the customer does not read the code.",
   ex:["Errors nest at the boundaries, almost never in the middle.",
       "Covering every line is not enough: each branch must be taken both ways."]},
29:{s:"Four ways to organise development, and what each one assumes",
   e:"The waterfall model chains phases with no going back: it requires clear requirements from the start and makes late change very costly. Prototyping builds a mock-up to settle doubts before committing. The spiral model repeats cycles, handling the biggest risks first. Agile methods deliver something working in short iterations and accept that requirements evolve.",
   t:"Agile is not « no documentation » or « no plan »: it is a short delivery rhythm with feedback at every iteration. And the V-model is a waterfall variant, not an iterative approach.",
   ex:["Its weakness — no going back — is not one if nothing moves.",
       "You discover the need by delivering, instead of guessing it upfront."]},
30:{s:"Finding the path that decides the whole project duration",
   e:"The arrow diagram shows the tasks and their dependencies. The critical path is the longest in cumulative duration: it sets the project duration, and none of its tasks has any slack. Tasks off that path have float: they can slip without delaying the end. Shortening the project therefore means shortening a task on the critical path, not any other.",
   t:"Shortening a non-critical task gains nothing. And beware: speeding up the critical path can make another path become the longest — the critical path moves.",
   ex:["It is always the longest, never the shortest.",
       "No slack on that path: any delay passes straight through."]},
31:{s:"Committing to a service level, and knowing who does what when it breaks",
   e:"The service level agreement states in writing what is promised: availability, response time, support hours. Incident management aims to restore service as fast as possible, even through a workaround. Problem management hunts the root cause so the incident does not return. They are two distinct jobs with different goals.",
   t:"Incident and problem are constantly mixed up: the incident is the interruption the user experiences, the problem is its cause. Restoring fast and understanding why can even conflict in the heat of the moment.",
   ex:["The goal is restoration, not explanation.",
       "The goal is that it does not happen again."]},
32:{s:"Having the system checked by someone who did not build it",
   e:"A systems audit assesses whether the system is reliable, secure and effective. The key point is independence: the auditor must not have taken part in what they are checking, otherwise they judge themselves. They gather evidence, document findings and issue a report with recommendations — but they do not fix anything: correction belongs to the audited department, and the auditor later checks it happened.",
   t:"The auditor does not repair, they observe and recommend. And they need not come from outside — independence can be internal (another department) or external (a firm).",
   ex:["You cannot be both judge and party.",
       "The auditor recommends; they do not execute."]},
33:{s:"The analysis grids you will be asked to recognise",
   e:"SWOT crosses two axes: internal (strengths, weaknesses) and external (opportunities, threats). The portfolio matrix classifies activities by market growth and market share, giving four boxes: star, cash cow, question mark, dog. The 3C analysis looks at customer, competitor and company. Questions almost always ask which axis a given item belongs to.",
   t:"The constant confusion is internal versus external. What the company owns or controls is internal (strength or weakness); what comes from the market, competitors or regulation is external (opportunity or threat).",
   ex:["Internal and favourable: a strength, not an opportunity.",
       "External and unfavourable: a threat, not a weakness."]},
34:{s:"The revenue at which you stop losing money",
   e:"Costs split in two: fixed costs, which do not move with volume (rent, salaries), and variable costs, proportional to sales (materials, commissions). The break-even point is the revenue at which profit is exactly zero. You compute it by dividing fixed costs by the contribution margin ratio, that is by one minus the variable cost ratio.",
   t:"The mistake is dividing by the variable cost ratio instead of its complement. Check: the break-even point is always above the fixed costs. If your answer is lower, you divided by 0.6 instead of 0.4.",
   ex:["Below it you lose, above it you earn.",
       "Raising fixed costs mechanically raises the threshold."]},
35:{s:"What is protected, by which right, and from when",
   e:"Copyright protects a concrete expression — a written program, a text, an image — and arises automatically on creation, with no filing. It protects neither the idea, nor the algorithm as such, nor a programming language. A patent protects an invention, but only after filing and examination. The unauthorised access law forbids logging in with someone else’s credentials, even without causing damage.",
   t:"Three points recur: copyright needs no registration, it does not cover ideas, and using someone else’s credentials is an offence in itself, damage or not. And a work created by an employee in the course of their job belongs in principle to the employer.",
   ex:["Protection exists from the moment it is written, with no formality.",
       "To protect a technical idea you must go through a patent."]},
36:{s:"The management acronyms, and what each claims to solve",
   e:"These questions turn on a single word. ERP integrates the company’s resources (accounting, stock, staff) into one system to avoid scattered data. CRM manages the customer relationship over time to build loyalty. SCM drives the supply chain from supplier to end customer. SFA supports the sales activity. BPR does not merely computerise: it rebuilds the business process itself.",
   t:"The trap is confusing computerising with redesigning: BPR questions the process before putting a tool on it. And ERP and SCM often overlap in the wording — ERP looks inside the company, SCM looks along the chain beyond it.",
   ex:["Integration is the key word: a single source of data.",
       "Computerising a bad process only speeds up the mess."]},
37:{s:"Handling true/false conditions without getting the negation wrong",
   e:"The basic operations are AND (true only if both are), OR (true if at least one is), exclusive OR (true if they differ) and NOT. De Morgan’s laws say how to push a negation inside an expression: negating an AND gives an OR of negations, and the other way round. It is the most tested point here, and also the one most often got wrong when programming.",
   t:"When negating, flip the operator too: writing NOT A AND NOT B to negate « A or B » is right, NOT A OR NOT B is wrong. And do not confuse OR (inclusive, true when both are true) with exclusive OR (false when both are true).",
   ex:["Negating an OR turns the operator into an AND.",
       "Exclusive OR with itself cancels out: that is what parity bits rely on."]},
38:{s:"Why a busy system collapses long before it is full",
   e:"The simplest model assumes random arrivals and a single server. Utilisation is the share of time the server is busy. Mean waiting time grows not linearly but as ρ/(1−ρ): while utilisation stays low the wait is negligible; as it approaches 1 the wait explodes. That is the technical reason nobody sizes a server for 95 % load.",
   t:"The mistake is reasoning linearly: going from 50 % to 80 % load does not multiply the wait by 1.6 but by four. And response time is the wait « plus » the service, not the wait alone.",
   ex:["At half load you wait as long as the service itself takes.",
       "Utilisation rose by a factor of 1.6, the wait by a factor of four."]},
39:{s:"Turning a clock frequency into instructions per second",
   e:"The clock frequency gives cycles per second; its inverse is the duration of one cycle. Cycles per instruction says how many cycles an instruction costs. Multiplying the two gives the time for one instruction, and its inverse gives instructions per second, expressed in MIPS. The unit trap is the same as in networking: giga, mega and nano.",
   t:"A higher frequency does not mean a faster machine: it depends on cycles per instruction and on the instruction set. Comparing two processors of different architectures by GHz alone is meaningless.",
   ex:["Two billion cycles per second, four cycles per instruction.",
       "Halving the cycles per instruction doubles performance without touching the clock."]},
40:{s:"How the system advances several tasks with one processor",
   e:"When a task waits for input or output the processor would sit idle, so the system runs another task meanwhile. A task moves between three states — running, ready to run, waiting for an event. The scheduler picks which one runs, by priority, by arrival order, or round-robin giving each a time slice.",
   t:"« Waiting » and « ready » are different states: ready means only the processor is missing, waiting means something else is (a disk read, a keystroke). Only ready tasks are candidates to run.",
   ex:["The processor never waits: it switches.",
       "Each in turn, for a fixed slice: nobody monopolises."]},
41:{s:"How an addition is built out of logic gates",
   e:"Gates implement logic operations in hardware. The half adder adds two bits: exclusive OR gives the sum, AND gives the carry. It cannot take an incoming carry into account, hence the full adder, built from two half adders and an OR gate, which adds two bits plus the previous carry. Chaining full adders adds n-bit numbers.",
   t:"Do not confuse the two: the half adder has two inputs, the full adder three (the two bits and the incoming carry). Only the second can be chained.",
   ex:["Those two gates are enough to describe one-bit addition.",
       "That is what allows adders to be chained across several bits."]},
42:{s:"Which memory does what, and which one forgets when power goes",
   e:"Dynamic RAM stores information in tiny capacitors that leak, so it must be refreshed constantly. It is dense and cheap, hence its use as main memory. Static RAM needs no refresh, is far faster but expensive and bulky: that is cache memory. Both are volatile. Flash memory keeps its contents without power and can be rewritten: USB sticks, SSDs, phone storage.",
   t:"« Static » does not mean non-volatile: SRAM is volatile and loses everything at power-off, just like DRAM. What sets it apart is the absence of refresh. Only flash and ROM keep data unpowered.",
   ex:["Dense and cheap: you fit a lot of it.",
       "Fast and expensive: you fit a little, right next to the processor."]},
43:{s:"Designing so it can be used, and used by everyone",
   e:"Usability measures ease of use for the intended user: speed of learning, efficiency, low error rate. Accessibility widens the question to those with limitations — sight, hearing, movement, age — demanding for instance sufficient contrast, a text alternative to images, navigation without a mouse. Universal design goes further: design for the widest range from the start rather than adapting afterwards.",
   t:"Accessibility is not « for disabled people only »: good contrast also helps in bright sunlight, and subtitles help on a train. That is also why universal design differs from a specific adaptation.",
   ex:["Information must never rest on colour alone.",
       "Preventing the error beats reporting it after submission."]},
44:{s:"Turning the real world into bits, and shrinking it with or without loss",
   e:"An image breaks down into pixels, each coded on a number of bits: 24 bits give about 16.7 million colours. Raw size is therefore pixel count times depth. Lossless compression recovers the original exactly, which suits text and programs. Lossy compression discards what the eye or ear barely notices: the reduction is far better, but the original is gone for good.",
   t:"Never apply lossy compression to data where every bit counts: an executable or a database tolerates no approximation. And mind the units: the formula yields bits, the answer is nearly always expected in bytes.",
   ex:["Divide by eight at the end, to go from bits to bytes.",
       "A program with one bit changed no longer runs: loss is forbidden there."]},
45:{s:"The order of the documents when calling on a supplier",
   e:"The buyer starts with a request for information, to learn what exists on the market and which suppliers could answer. They then write a request for proposal describing the need, the constraints and the selection criteria, and send it to the shortlisted candidates. Those return a proposal, which is assessed against criteria announced in advance, and the contract goes to the winner.",
   t:"The order is regularly reversed in questions: information first, proposal second. And the evaluation criteria must appear in the request for proposal, not be decided after the offers arrive.",
   ex:["No offer is being asked for yet: this is fact-finding.",
       "The need is formalised: priced proposals are requested."]},
46:{s:"How a technology spreads, and where it most often dies",
   e:"Adopters of a novelty fall into five groups, from the boldest to the most reluctant. Between the early adopters and the early majority lies a chasm: the first buy out of appetite for novelty, the majority only buys once the product is reliable, useful and safe. Many promising technologies stop there. Product innovation creates a new offer; process innovation improves the way of producing.",
   t:"The difficulty is commercial, not technical: crossing the chasm means changing the pitch, not improving the product further. And do not confuse product with process innovation — questions often turn on exactly that.",
   ex:["What convinced the first buyers does not convince the next ones.",
       "Innovation does not always concern the product itself."]},
47:{s:"What the buzzwords actually cover, and where to place them",
   e:"The internet of things connects sensors and equipment that report data continuously. Those volumes feed big data, characterised by quantity, variety and speed of arrival. Machine learning builds models from them: supervised when the right answers are supplied in advance, unsupervised when the point is to discover groupings, reinforcement when the system learns by trial and reward.",
   t:"Supervised learning needs data that is already labelled, and that is usually where a project’s real cost sits — not in the algorithm. And IoT is not just connectivity: without processing the data that comes back, no connected object delivers value.",
   ex:["Each example comes with its expected label.",
       "The grouping emerges from the data, with no reference answer."]},
48:{s:"Doubling the hardware, and choosing what the second copy does",
   e:"A dual system runs two identical chains in parallel and compares the results: an error is caught immediately, at the cost of permanently doubled hardware. A duplex system keeps a main system and a standby. If the standby is already running and takes over in seconds, that is hot standby; if it must be started first, that is cold standby — cheaper but slower.",
   t:"Dual and duplex are constantly mixed up: dual runs the same work twice to « compare », duplex keeps a second system to « replace ». And cold standby costs less but recovery is measured in minutes, not seconds.",
   ex:["Comparison detects the error; it does not merely replace.",
       "The standby draws power all the time, but switching is almost instant."]},
49:{s:"Spreading data over several disks, for speed or for safety",
   e:"Level 0 splits the data across disks and writes in parallel: fast, but one disk failure loses everything. Level 1 writes the same content to two disks: you survive a failure, but half the capacity is spent. Level 5 spreads data and parity over at least three disks: a lost disk can be rebuilt, and usable capacity is that of n−1 disks.",
   t:"Level 0 provides no redundancy despite the RAID name: it actually raises the risk, since one failure loses the lot. And RAID 5 tolerates only one simultaneous failure — lose two disks and the data goes with them.",
   ex:["One whole disk is spent on parity, but spread across all four.",
       "Mirroring halves the capacity: that is the price of safety."]},
50:{s:"In what order to walk a tree, and what each order is for",
   e:"A binary tree can be walked three ways, differing only in when the node itself is handled. Preorder handles the node before its children: useful to copy a structure. Inorder handles it between its two children: on a binary search tree it produces the values in sorted order. Postorder handles it after its children: useful to evaluate an expression or free a tree.",
   t:"The position of « order » in the name gives it away: pre = node first, in = node in the middle, post = node last. Subtrees are always walked left to right, whichever order is used.",
   ex:["That is the most useful property of a binary search tree.",
       "Operands come out before the operator, exactly as in postfix notation."]},
51:{s:"Interrupting cleanly, and overlapping instructions",
   e:"An interrupt suspends the current work, saves its state, runs a routine, then resumes where it stopped. It is internal when the cause lies in the program itself — division by zero, overflow — and external when it comes from outside — a disk read finishing, a timer. Pipelining splits instruction execution into stages and overlaps successive instructions, like an assembly line.",
   t:"Pipelining improves throughput, not the duration of a single instruction. And a branch breaks the flow: instructions already in the pipe must be discarded, cancelling part of the gain. Hence branch prediction.",
   ex:["The cause lies in the running program.",
       "The cause lies outside the program."]},
52:{s:"Drawing the data structure before writing a single table",
   e:"The entity-relationship model shows the things you want to track and the links between them, with cardinality: one to one, one to many, many to many. A many-to-many relation cannot be translated directly into relational tables: it is broken up with an intermediate table carrying both foreign keys. That is the operation most often asked about.",
   t:"The rule: in a one-to-many relation the foreign key goes on the « many » side, never the other way. And a many-to-many relation always requires an extra table — it cannot be done with two crossed foreign keys.",
   ex:["The intermediate table carries both keys and often its own data, such as a grade.",
       "The « many » side carries the foreign key."]},
53:{s:"How several machines share one medium",
   e:"On a wired local network, machines listen before sending and detect collisions when two send at once: that is collision detection. Over the air a station cannot listen while transmitting, so the aim is to avoid collisions rather than detect them. Address translation lets several machines on a private network reach the Internet behind a single public address.",
   t:"CSMA/CD and CSMA/CA differ by one letter: detection for wired, avoidance for wireless. The reason is physical — a wireless card cannot listen and transmit at the same time.",
   ex:["Detection happens after the fact, then each retries after a random wait.",
       "Private addresses are not routable on the Internet: they must be translated."]},
54:{s:"What an organisation puts in place before and after the incident",
   e:"The incident response team is formed in advance to detect, analyse and handle attacks, and to coordinate communication. The business continuity plan sets out how to keep essential activities running after a major disaster, with a target recovery time. The security management system is the permanent framework: policy, risk analysis, measures, review, improvement — the PDCA cycle applied to security.",
   t:"Do not confuse continuity, which keeps the business running through a crisis, with disaster recovery, which gets the system back up. And a management system is not a document: it is a cycle that has to keep turning.",
   ex:["Formed before the incident, not improvised during it.",
       "The point is not to prevent the disaster but to survive what happens."]},
55:{s:"Estimating before starting, then measuring the gap along the way",
   e:"Function point estimation starts from what the software does — inputs, outputs, files, enquiries — weighted by complexity, rather than from lines of code, which depend on the language. During the project, earned value compares three amounts: what was planned, what has actually been produced, and what has been spent. Cost variance is earned value minus actual cost; schedule variance is earned value minus planned value.",
   t:"Both variances start from earned value: only the term subtracted changes. Negative is bad in both cases. And estimating in lines of code misleads, since a more expressive language produces fewer of them for the same work.",
   ex:["Negative: more was spent than was produced.",
       "Negative: less was produced than was planned by that date."]},
56:{s:"The graphical quality tools, and the question each one answers",
   e:"The Pareto chart ranks causes by decreasing importance with a cumulative line: it shows that a few causes explain most of the defects. The cause-and-effect diagram, shaped like a fishbone, groups possible causes by family. The histogram shows the distribution of values. The scatter diagram shows correlation between two quantities. The control chart follows production over time and flags variation outside the limits.",
   t:"The common confusion is histogram (distribution of one quantity) versus scatter diagram (relation between two). And a Pareto chart only works sorted in decreasing order: unsorted, it loses its whole point.",
   ex:["You tackle the causes that weigh the most first.",
       "It shows correlation, which is not causation."]},
57:{s:"Who gives the orders, and who answers for the result",
   e:"Under a contract for work, the provider commits to a result and directs their own staff: the client may not instruct them directly. Under staff dispatch it is the opposite: the host company directs the daily work, but nobody commits to a deliverable. Giving direct orders to a subcontractor’s staff while under a contract for work is disguised dispatch, which is illegal.",
   t:"It always comes back to two points: who directs the work, and is there a commitment on the result. Contract for work: the provider directs and answers for the result. Dispatch: the client directs and nobody answers for the result.",
   ex:["The form of the contract no longer matches the reality of the work.",
       "Under dispatch, nobody commits to a deliverable."]},
58:{s:"Combining probabilities without picking the wrong operation",
   e:"Two independent events that must both happen: multiply their probabilities. Two mutually exclusive events where either will do: add them. The expected value is the sum of each possible value times its probability: the average you would get by repeating the experiment many times. In computing it underpins reliability calculations and sizing.",
   t:"« At least one » is not an addition: go through the complement, that is one minus the probability that none occurs. It is exactly the parallel availability calculation under another name.",
   ex:["« And » becomes a multiplication.",
       "« At least one » is computed from the complement of « none »."]},
59:{s:"Naming a file unambiguously, from the root or from where you are",
   e:"Files live in a tree of directories. An absolute path always starts from the root, so it names the same file wherever you write it from. A relative path starts from the current directory: one dot means the current directory, two dots the parent. The exam gives a tree and asks for the path from one file to another.",
   t:"Count the `..` carefully: one per level to climb, up to the common ancestor, then back down. The usual error is one too many or one too few.",
   ex:["You climb one level with .., then go back down.",
       "An absolute path does not depend on where you stand."]},
60:{s:"What an open licence guarantees, and what it does not forbid",
   e:"Open-source software gives access to the source and allows use, modification and redistribution, including commercially. Being free of charge is not part of the definition: open-source software may be sold. Some licences, called copyleft, require any distributed derivative to be released under the same licence; permissive ones merely require crediting the author. There is, however, no warranty that it works.",
   t:"Two stubborn misconceptions: open does not mean free of charge, and open does not mean without an author or without rights. The author keeps their copyright — that is precisely what lets them impose the licence terms.",
   ex:["Freedom is forcibly passed on to derived versions.",
       "Very few constraints, but no obligation to give back."]},
61:{s:"Organising information so it is understood, not merely displayed",
   e:"Designing information means structuring it, making it visible and keeping only what matters. You group what belongs together, rank by importance, and pick the form that carries the message best: a table to compare, a curve for a trend, a pictogram to be understood without words. The aim is not beauty but fast, error-free understanding.",
   t:"Decorating is not designing: colours and effects without logic hurt readability. And a badly chosen chart — a pie chart for a trend over time, say — makes the information harder to read than a plain table.",
   ex:["Understandable without reading the local language.",
       "The form follows the question asked, not personal taste."]},
62:{s:"The techniques of moving images and synthetic worlds",
   e:"Digital video compresses by exploiting the similarity between successive frames: only the differences are transmitted, hence the MPEG standards. In computer graphics, modelling defines the shapes, rendering computes the final image with lights and textures, interpolation generates the in-between frames of an animation. Virtual reality immerses the user in a fully computed world; augmented reality overlays information on the real one.",
   t:"Do not confuse virtual with augmented reality: the first replaces the world, the second adds to it. And video compression is nearly always lossy — reproducing the exact original is not the goal.",
   ex:["Two consecutive frames look alike: coding the difference is enough.",
       "The real world stays visible: a layer is added on top."]},
63:{s:"Knowing at any moment which version is authoritative, and what may be changed",
   e:"A project produces dozens of documents and files evolving in parallel. Configuration management identifies the items concerned, records their versions and tracks who changed what, when and why. A baseline is an approved state used as a reference: beyond it, any change goes through a change request, assessed for impact before being accepted.",
   t:"Configuration management is not a backup: it is not for recovering after a crash but for knowing which version is the reference at all times. And a baseline does not freeze the project — it only requires assessment before changing.",
   ex:["From then on nothing is changed freely: a change request is raised.",
       "Being able to go back assumes knowing where you came from."]},
64:{s:"Speaking the same language as the supplier, and measuring their maturity",
   e:"The common framework describes the activities of a software life cycle — planning, acquisition, development, operation, maintenance — with shared vocabulary. Its purpose is not to impose a method but to stop client and supplier from meaning different things by the word « design ». The maturity model instead rates how well an organisation masters its processes, from an initial unpredictable level up to continuous optimisation.",
   t:"The common framework is not a development method: it does not say how to do things, it defines what is being talked about. And the maturity assessed is that of the organisation over time, not the quality of one project.",
   ex:["Most disputes come from one word understood differently.",
       "What is rated is the organisation, not a particular project."]},
65:{s:"Three ways to back up, which do not cost the same to restore",
   e:"A full backup copies everything: slow to make, simple to restore. A differential backup copies what has changed since the last full one: it grows each day, but restoring needs only the full one plus the latest differential. An incremental backup copies what has changed since the last backup of any kind: fastest to produce, but restoring needs the full one plus every incremental in order.",
   t:"The trade-off is always the same: incremental saves time when backing up and costs time when restoring; differential does the opposite. And restoration must itself be tested — an untested backup is not a backup.",
   ex:["Two files are enough, whatever the day.",
       "Each incremental holds only one link of the chain."]},
66:{s:"The single desk for users, and what keeps the building running",
   e:"The service desk is the users’ single point of contact: it logs requests, qualifies them, resolves or escalates them, and reports progress. On the facilities side, the uninterruptible power supply keeps the power on long enough to shut down cleanly or start a generator. Two figures frame recovery: how soon the service must be back, and how old the recovered data may be.",
   t:"The two objectives are regularly confused: one is about downtime, the other about data lost. A one-hour recovery point objective forces hourly backups, whatever the recovery time objective.",
   ex:["How long the interruption may last.",
       "How much work you accept to lose — that dictates backup frequency."]},
67:{s:"Organising work so that fraud and error become hard",
   e:"Internal control rests on organisation more than technology. Separation of duties means one person cannot both commit, approve and pay an expense. Mutual checking has one person’s work verified by another. IT governance is the board’s responsibility: making sure IT serves the strategy and that risks are held.",
   t:"Internal control is not about tools: no software makes up for an organisation where the same person decides, executes and checks. And audit sits outside internal control — it verifies that it works.",
   ex:["Fraud would then require two people to collude.",
       "This is not an IT department matter but a board one."]},
68:{s:"How far you hand your computing over to the provider",
   e:"The three forms differ by what the customer still has to manage. With infrastructure as a service you rent machines and network, and install everything else yourself. With platform as a service the operating system and runtime come included: you bring only your application. With software as a service you use a ready-made application, installing nothing. The higher you go, the less you manage and the less you can adapt.",
   t:"The classification always follows what the customer still manages: the more they manage, the lower in the stack. And outsourcing does not remove responsibility — personal data protection stays the customer’s even when hosted elsewhere.",
   ex:["The provider stops at virtualised hardware.",
       "Nothing to install, nothing to administer, but little to tune."]},
69:{s:"Drawing how information flows before deciding what to computerise",
   e:"The data flow diagram shows where information comes from, who processes it and where it is stored, without saying when or how. It distinguishes four elements: processes, data stores, entities outside the system, and the flows linking them. It is used to understand the existing situation before redesigning it — the step that precedes BPR.",
   t:"A data flow diagram shows neither time order nor conditions: it is not a program flowchart. Trying to put branches in it is the classic mistake.",
   ex:["A named arrow: it says what information moves, not how.",
       "What lies outside the studied scope is still shown, at the edge."]},
70:{s:"Judging a project on its full cost, not its purchase price",
   e:"Total cost of ownership adds up everything a system costs over its life: purchase, but also installation, training, operation, maintenance, power and disposal. The purchase price is often only a fraction. Return on investment relates the gain to the amount invested, and the payback period says after how long the investment is repaid by annual gains.",
   t:"Comparing two solutions on purchase price alone nearly always leads to the wrong choice: the cheapest to buy is frequently the most expensive to run. That is exactly what total cost of ownership is for.",
   ex:["Two thirds of the cost come after the purchase.",
       "Beyond four years the project pays off."]},
71:{s:"Three ways to compete, which cannot all be pursued at once",
   e:"Cost leadership seeks the lowest cost base, through volume and efficiency. Differentiation seeks higher perceived value — quality, brand, service — justifying a higher price. Focus gives up the broad market to serve a narrow segment, either on cost or on differentiation. Trying to lead on the first two at once usually means excelling at neither.",
   t:"Questions often turn on that incompatibility: a company trying to be both cheapest and most differentiated ends up « stuck in the middle ». And focus is not a middle road: it is the choice of a narrower ground.",
   ex:["Volume is the weapon, so a broad market is needed.",
       "Volume is given up for a better fit."]},
72:{s:"The four commercial levers, and where value is created inside the firm",
   e:"The marketing mix describes the four variables a company acts on: which product, at what price, through which channel, with what communication. The value chain splits the company into activities and asks which ones genuinely add value for the customer: primary activities follow the product from supply to after-sales, support activities (human resources, technology, procurement, infrastructure) cut across them all.",
   t:"The four Ps are seen from the company’s side; the four Cs restate the same from the customer’s (value, cost, convenience, communication). And in the value chain, support activities are not secondary — they condition all the others.",
   ex:["Four levers, rarely pulled all at once.",
       "You look for where value is created, and where it is not."]},
73:{s:"Three ways for a technique to become the reference",
   e:"A de jure standard is set by an official body through a procedure — ISO, JIS, IEC. A de facto standard imposes itself because the market adopted it massively, with no official decision. A forum standard is defined by an industry consortium, faster than the official route. In fast-moving technologies the de facto standard often precedes the de jure one.",
   t:"A de facto standard has no legal force and can be replaced if the market shifts. That is also why companies fight to impose theirs — winning the standard means winning the market.",
   ex:["Nobody decreed it: users imposed it.",
       "An official procedure, slow but enforceable."]},
74:{s:"Who sells to whom, and how firms exchange documents",
   e:"Electronic commerce is classified first by the parties involved: between businesses, from a business to a consumer, or between consumers on a marketplace. Electronic data interchange lets companies send orders and invoices in an agreed format, with no re-keying. On the payment side, electronic money and settlement services join the bank card, with strong authentication requirements.",
   t:"Electronic data interchange is not email with an attachment: what defines it is an agreed structured format, read directly by the systems on both sides with no human intervention.",
   ex:["Format and protocol agreed in advance between partners.",
       "The platform introduces the parties without being party to the sale."]},
75:{s:"Producing without useless stock, and running computers inside objects",
   e:"Just-in-time triggers production from downstream: you make only what has just been consumed, which cuts stock but leaves the chain sensitive to disruption. Material requirements planning instead starts from sales forecasts and computes the quantities needed. Embedded systems are computers built into equipment — vehicles, appliances, sensors — under real-time, memory and power constraints.",
   t:"Just-in-time cuts stock but adds fragility: a late supplier stops the line, since there is no buffer. It is the classic trade-off between holding cost and robustness.",
   ex:["Downstream gives the order, not the forecast.",
       "The user often does not even know a computer is in there."]},
76:{s:"Counting elements without counting any twice",
   e:"Set operations match logic exactly: union is OR, intersection is AND, complement is NOT. The counting trap is double counting: elements in both sets are counted once in each, so the size of the union is the sum of the sizes minus the intersection. These questions are nearly always solved by drawing the circles.",
   t:"The everyday « or » is often exclusive, while union is not: « A or B » in mathematics includes those in both. And « neither one nor the other » is the complement of the union, not the sum of the complements.",
   ex:["Without subtracting the intersection, those ten would be counted twice.",
       "A complement is always read against the whole set."]},
77:{s:"Why a computer gets it wrong while calculating correctly",
   e:"A real number has only finitely many bits to be written in: what overflows is lost, and that is rounding error. Subtracting two very close values cancels the shared significant digits and leaves little but noise: that is cancellation. Adding a very large and a very small number makes the small one vanish, far below the available precision: that is loss of significance. All three follow from the encoding, not from bugs.",
   t:"To limit loss of significance, add the small values together before adding the large ones. To avoid cancellation, rewrite the expression — for instance by multiplying by the conjugate — rather than subtracting directly.",
   ex:["Six shared digits cancel out, leaving one unreliable digit.",
       "The small term falls outside the precision window."]},
78:{s:"Writing a calculation without brackets, so a machine can evaluate it",
   e:"In postfix notation the operator follows its two operands: A + B is written A B +. Since the order is carried by position, brackets disappear. Evaluation uses a stack: push the values, and at each operator pop the last two, compute, push the result. It is exactly the postorder walk of the expression tree.",
   t:"Converting by hand is safest by drawing the expression tree and walking it in postorder. Trying to shuffle operators around leads to errors as soon as precedence is involved.",
   ex:["The sum is computed first, so its operator comes before the ×.",
       "Multiplication binds tighter: it reduces before the addition."]},
79:{s:"Describing a language’s grammar in a few lines",
   e:"Backus-Naur form defines syntax through replacement rules. The definition symbol introduces what an element stands for, the vertical bar separates alternatives, and a rule referring to itself expresses repetition: a number is a digit, or a number followed by a digit. The exam gives rules and asks which strings they accept.",
   t:"The reliable method is to unfold the rule step by step on the proposed string rather than judging by eye. And a recursive rule must always have a non-recursive base case, otherwise it generates nothing.",
   ex:["The second rule is recursive: it generates numbers of any length.",
       "No rule contains a dot, so a dot is not acceptable."]},
80:{s:"The structure that opposes the array point for point",
   e:"In a linked list, each element holds its value and the address of the next. Inserting or deleting only changes two links, with nothing to move: it is immediate. But reaching the n-th element means starting from the beginning and following the links one by one. The array is exactly the reverse: direct access by index, but costly insertion since the following elements must be shifted.",
   t:"There is no best structure in the abstract: if you traverse a lot and insert little, the array wins; if you insert and delete in the middle often, the list wins. The exam question always states the dominant use.",
   ex:["The choice depends on what you do most often.",
       "Direct access is what the list cannot do."]},
81:{s:"Near-instant access, provided collisions are handled",
   e:"A hash function computes directly the slot where a value belongs from its key: access does not depend on the number of elements. But two different keys can land on the same slot. Chaining then stores both in a list attached to that slot. Open addressing looks for another free slot by a fixed rule. The fuller the table, the more collisions and the worse the performance.",
   t:"Collisions cannot be removed, only made rare: that is why the table is always sized larger than the expected number of elements. And the advertised O(1) assumes a moderate load factor.",
   ex:["A collision: a strategy is needed, it cannot be avoided.",
       "The slot now holds the head of a list rather than a value."]},
82:{s:"Why doubling the cores does not halve the time",
   e:"A multicore processor holds several computing units able to work at once. But every program has a part that cannot be parallelised — sequential reading, dependencies between steps. That part sets the ceiling: even with infinitely many cores, total time cannot drop below it. That is why real gains fall well short of the core count.",
   t:"The mistake is assuming performance follows the number of cores. And beyond a point, coordination between cores — synchronisation, shared memory — costs time itself and can wipe out the gain.",
   ex:["A fifth of the work stays sequential whatever you do.",
       "The core count only helps the parallelisable part."]},
83:{s:"Moving data between the processor and the outside world",
   e:"Serial transmission sends bits one after another on a single wire; parallel sends several at once on separate wires. Counter-intuitively serial won — USB, SATA — because at high frequency parallel wires drift out of step. Direct memory access lets a device write to memory without going through the processor, which can work on something else during the transfer.",
   t:"Parallel does not mean faster: beyond a certain frequency, signals on different wires no longer arrive together. That is exactly why modern interfaces are all serial.",
   ex:["The processor is only notified at the end, by an interrupt.",
       "At high speed, one well-controlled wire beats several drifting ones."]},
84:{s:"Two ways of making a machine execute a text",
   e:"A compiler translates the whole program into machine code before anything runs: translation takes time but execution is fast, and syntax errors show up at compile time. An interpreter reads and runs statement by statement: startup is immediate and debugging more flexible, but execution is slower and an error only appears when that line is reached. Compilation goes through lexical analysis, parsing, semantic analysis, optimisation and code generation.",
   t:"It is not the language that is compiled or interpreted, it is its implementation: one language can have both. And virtual machines mix the approaches — compile to intermediate code, then interpret or compile on the fly.",
   ex:["A syntax error is reported before the program is even launched.",
       "The error on line 500 only shows once line 500 is reached."]},
85:{s:"What sits between the system and the application, and how you plug into it",
   e:"Middleware provides services many applications need, so each need not reimplement them: database management, web server, message queue. An application programming interface is the contract by which those services are called: you know what to send and what comes back, without knowing the internals. That is what lets the implementation be replaced without touching the callers.",
   t:"The whole point of an interface is to hide the inside: as long as the contract holds, the provider can rewrite everything. Conversely, depending on an external interface creates a dependency — if it changes or disappears, the application falls.",
   ex:["Neither operating system nor business application: in between.",
       "You use the function without knowing how it is written."]},
86:{s:"The two ends through which computing touches the physical world",
   e:"A sensor converts a physical quantity — temperature, light, acceleration, pressure — into an electrical signal the system can read. An actuator does the reverse: it turns a command into movement or physical action, through a motor, a valve, a cylinder. Processing sits between the two: read, decide, act. That is the basic loop of every embedded system and connected object.",
   t:"The two are regularly swapped in questions: the sensor measures, the actuator acts. Feeding the sensor reading back into the command creates closed-loop control, correcting continuously rather than acting blind.",
   ex:["From the physical world into information.",
       "From information into the physical world."]},
87:{s:"The difference between a circuit that computes and one that remembers",
   e:"A combinational circuit produces an output depending only on the present inputs: logic gates and adders are of that kind. A sequential circuit also depends on what happened before, which requires a memory element. The flip-flop is that basic element: it holds one bit until told to change. Registers and counters are built from flip-flops.",
   t:"There is a single test: does the circuit need to know its previous state? A counter does — it must know where it is. An adder does not — it recomputes everything each time.",
   ex:["No memory: the past does not exist for it.",
       "It is the memory element every register is made of."]},
88:{s:"Why text sometimes shows up as gibberish",
   e:"A character is a number, and the character set says which number means which sign. The historical American encoding covers Latin letters in seven bits. Japanese encodings added kana and kanji on two bytes, under several incompatible conventions. Unicode gives every character in the world a unique number, and UTF-8 encodes it on one to four bytes while staying compatible with ASCII for Latin characters.",
   t:"Gibberish does not mean the data is lost: the file is intact, only the reading table is wrong. And in UTF-8 the size of a text cannot be deduced from the number of characters, which matters for field limits in databases.",
   ex:["The bytes are intact: it is their interpretation that differs.",
       "Length varies by character: that is the whole point of variable-length encoding."]},
89:{s:"Speeding up searches, and knowing when the relational model stops fitting",
   e:"An index is a side structure letting rows be found without scanning the whole table, like a book index. It speeds up reading but slows writing, since it must be kept up to date on every change, and it takes space. Non-relational databases drop the table model for flexibility or distribution: key-value, documents, graphs, each suited to a particular use.",
   t:"Adding indexes is not free and does not speed up everything: on a very small table a full scan is often faster. And non-relational databases do not replace relational ones — they answer other constraints, often at the price of weaker consistency.",
   ex:["The read gain far outweighs the write cost.",
       "Every write must update all the indexes concerned."]},
90:{s:"How the layers nest, and why addresses ran out",
   e:"On sending, each layer adds its header in front of the data received from the layer above: that is encapsulation. On receiving, each layer strips its own and passes the rest upward. On addressing, the thirty-two bits of version 4 no longer suffice for the number of connected devices; version 6 takes the address to one hundred and twenty-eight bits, settling the shortage for a long time and removing the need for address translation.",
   t:"Encapsulation explains why the same byte is « data » to one layer and « header » to another: it depends on the point of view. And address translation was only a stopgap for the IPv4 shortage — with IPv6 it has no reason to exist.",
   ex:["Like nested envelopes, opened in reverse order.",
       "Going from 32 to 128 bits is not four times more: it is inconceivably more."]},
91:{s:"Which diagram answers which question",
   e:"The unified modelling language offers several complementary views of the same system. The class diagram shows structure: which entities exist and how they relate, with no notion of time. The sequence diagram shows instead the chronology of exchanges between objects. The use case diagram describes what the system offers seen from outside, by actor. The state diagram follows an object’s successive states and the events that change them.",
   t:"The choice is simple: if the question is about « what exists », it is a structure diagram; if about « in what order », a behaviour diagram. And a use case diagram never describes the inside of the system, only what can be asked of it.",
   ex:["Structure and cardinalities, with no notion of time.",
       "The vertical axis is time: that is what sets it apart."]},
92:{s:"Splitting a program so it stays modifiable",
   e:"Cohesion measures how far a module’s parts serve one single task: the best is functional cohesion, where the module does exactly one thing. Coupling measures how far modules depend on each other: the best is data coupling, passing only the values needed. The worst is content coupling, where one module reads or writes another’s internals. Good design aims for high cohesion and low coupling.",
   t:"The two go in opposite directions, which is the source of error: you want cohesion **high** and coupling **low**. A highly cohesive but tightly coupled module is still hard to change, and so is a loosely coupled catch-all one.",
   ex:["Each module ignores how the other is built.",
       "Any change in one breaks the other without warning."]},
93:{s:"Finding defects before execution, then living with the delivered software",
   e:"A review examines a document or code without running it. An informal review is led by the author; an inspection is formal, led by a third party, with defined roles and a written record. The earlier a defect is found, the less it costs. After delivery, maintenance falls into four families: fixing what does not work, adapting to environment changes, improving what already works, and preventing future failures.",
   t:"Corrective maintenance is usually not the most expensive over a lifetime: adaptation and improvement weigh more. And a review never runs the program — that is exactly what distinguishes it from a test.",
   ex:["The software worked: the world around it changed.",
       "Nothing is broken; you act before it is."]},
94:{s:"What surrounds the writing of code and sets its pace",
   e:"The integrated development environment brings together editor, compiler, debugger and often version control. Continuous integration goes further: on every change submitted, a server rebuilds the software and replays the tests automatically, so a regression surfaces in minutes rather than weeks. Defect tracking and version control tools complete the set.",
   t:"Continuous integration is only worth it with automated tests: without them the server checks nothing but compilation. And it does not replace review — it catches what breaks, not what is badly designed.",
   ex:["The shorter the gap between error and detection, the cheaper it is.",
       "The gain comes mostly from not switching tools at every step."]},
95:{s:"Breaking the work down so it can be estimated and tracked",
   e:"The work breakdown structure decomposes what is to be delivered into ever finer elements, down to work packages small enough to estimate, assign and track. What is not in it is not in scope: that is what lets unplanned additions be refused cleanly. When scope grows through successive small requests while neither deadline nor budget moves, that is scope creep.",
   t:"The breakdown lists deliverables, not a schedule: dates come afterwards, once the packages exist. And work absent from the structure is not an oversight to absorb quietly — it is a change request.",
   ex:["Small enough to estimate confidently, large enough to mean something.",
       "Each request looks tiny; it is their accumulation that kills the project."]},
96:{s:"Dealing with what has not happened yet",
   e:"A risk is an uncertain event that would affect the project if it occurred. You identify it, assess it by crossing probability and impact, then choose a response: remove the cause, hand the risk to a third party, reduce probability or impact, or accept it while keeping a reserve. You then monitor, since risks evolve. A problem is a risk that has materialised: it is no longer managed, it is handled.",
   t:"Do not confuse risk and problem: the first has not happened, the second has. And acceptance is not inaction — it assumes the cost was assessed and a reserve set aside.",
   ex:["You do not prevent the event, you move its consequence.",
       "Accepting is a decision, provided it has been costed."]},
97:{s:"The three constraints that pull against each other, and the people to keep informed",
   e:"Quality, cost and time form a triangle: improving one generally degrades at least one other, and it is for the sponsor to arbitrate, not the project manager alone. Stakeholders are everyone the project affects or who can affect it: client, users, management, subcontractors, sometimes legal. Identifying them early avoids late opposition from someone nobody thought to consult.",
   t:"The three constraints are not independent: promising better quality without touching deadline or budget nearly always means quietly sacrificing scope. Better to state the trade-off openly.",
   ex:["Adding people to a late project often makes it later.",
       "An opponent found late costs far more than one consulted early."]},
98:{s:"Changing a live service without breaking it",
   e:"Any change to a running system goes through assessment: what does it touch, what is the risk, what happens if it goes wrong. An advisory board decides. The release is then prepared: maintenance window, order of operations, checks, and above all a back-out procedure if the result is not what was expected. Applying a change with no back-out plan is the costliest mistake in operations.",
   t:"Change management and release management are distinct steps: the first decides whether to change, the second organises how. And even an emergency change must be recorded — otherwise nobody will know six months later why the system is in that state.",
   ex:["Deciding in advance when to give up stops you from fighting on all night.",
       "Urgency waives the wait, not the record."]},
99:{s:"Having enough resources tomorrow, and not stopping today",
   e:"Capacity management anticipates demand — users, data volume, traffic — and sizes resources accordingly, neither too late nor too early. Availability management aims for the service to be there when needed, through redundancy, monitoring and shorter repair times. Both are measured, forecast and negotiated in the service level agreement.",
   t:"Oversizing is not good capacity management: idle resources cost money. The aim is to follow demand, not to outrun it indefinitely — which is what makes cloud elasticity attractive.",
   ex:["Act before saturation, not after the complaints.",
       "You do not remove failures: you reduce their effect."]},
100:{s:"How an audit unfolds, and what it may rely on",
   e:"The auditor starts by planning: scope, objectives, schedule. The preliminary survey gives an overview from documents; the main survey gathers evidence through interviews, observation, control testing and examination of records. Findings go into working papers that justify every conclusion. The report presents findings and recommendations, and a follow-up then checks the corrections were made.",
   t:"The auditor cannot conclude on the auditee’s word alone: sufficient and appropriate evidence is required. And follow-up is part of the audit — a report nobody checks is worthless.",
   ex:["Evidence beats a statement.",
       "A finding with no written trace does not survive a challenge."]},
101:{s:"Forms of outsourcing, from renting space to full delegation",
   e:"A system integrator takes on building a system end to end. Colocation rents space and power for the customer’s own machines, which the customer still owns. Hosting rents the machines themselves. Outsourcing goes further and hands an entire activity — operations, support, sometimes a business process — to a provider. The more you delegate, the less you administer, but the greater the dependency.",
   t:"Colocation and hosting are constantly confused: the question is always « who owns the hardware ». And delegating never removes responsibility towards end customers or over personal data.",
   ex:["The hardware stays the customer’s: what is rented is space and power.",
       "The provider owns the machine; the customer uses it."]},
102:{s:"What happens before the first line of code is written",
   e:"You start by understanding how the work is actually done today, by observing and questioning those who do it. From that come the problems to solve, then a vision of what the system should bring, and finally a costed plan with scope, schedule, costs and expected benefits. Detailed requirements come only afterwards. Skipping the analysis of the existing leads to computerising dysfunction.",
   t:"Computerising the existing as-is improves nothing: if the process is poor, the system only makes it faster. That is exactly why BPR exists, and why analysis precedes design.",
   ex:["What people do often differs from what the procedure says.",
       "Without costing, the investment decision cannot be made."]},
103:{s:"How a company divides itself, and who decides what",
   e:"Functional organisation groups the same trades — production, sales, accounting — favouring expertise but making cross-cutting decisions slow. Divisional organisation groups by product or market, each division holding its own functions: more responsive, with duplicated means. Matrix organisation crosses the two and gives everyone two managers, improving allocation but creating authority conflicts. The chief information officer carries IT strategy at board level.",
   t:"A matrix structure mechanically creates dual reporting: that is what the exam tests, and also its practical limit. And the chief information officer is not the head of the IT department: the role is strategic, not operational.",
   ex:["Each division is almost a company within the company.",
       "Two chains of command: both its strength and its weakness."]},
104:{s:"Two documents, two different questions",
   e:"The balance sheet photographs the situation on a date: what the company owns on one side, how it was financed on the other, by debt or by equity. Both sides balance by construction. The income statement instead covers a period and details how revenue became the final result, successively deducting cost of sales, overheads, financial items and tax.",
   t:"The balance sheet is about an instant, the income statement about a duration: confusing them makes any question unanswerable. And profit is not cash — a profitable company can run short of money if its customers pay late.",
   ex:["First margin: what the activity brings in before overheads.",
       "This is the result of the business itself, excluding finance and one-offs."]},
105:{s:"Working-time rules, and what each contract form allows",
   e:"Labour law sets minimums a contract cannot undercut: maximum duration, rest, regulated overtime. The discretionary regime treats work as judged by result rather than hours, but applies only to roles defined by law. Flexitime lets the employee choose arrival and departure around a common core period. These are adjustments, not exemptions: rest and health protections remain.",
   t:"These regimes remove neither duration limits nor rest obligations: they cannot be used to work people without counting. And the discretionary regime cannot apply to any post — the law lists which.",
   ex:["A common core period stays mandatory so the team overlaps.",
       "Reserved to certain roles: it is not freely chosen."]},
106:{s:"A tree that keeps its values ordered at all times",
   e:"In a binary search tree, everything left of a node is smaller and everything right is larger. Searching means descending while comparing, discarding half at each level. Insertion works the same way, down to a free spot. Cost depends on height: if the tree stays balanced it is logarithmic; if it degenerates into a line, you are back to a linear scan.",
   t:"The advertised efficiency assumes a balanced tree, which is not automatic: inserting already-sorted data produces the worst possible case. That is why self-balancing trees exist.",
   ex:["Worst case: the tree becomes a list and search falls back to O(n).",
       "It is the immediate successor: the ordering is preserved."]},
107:{s:"Solving a big problem by reducing it to itself, smaller",
   e:"A recursive function calls itself on a reduced version of the problem until a base case solves directly. Without that base case, calls pile up until the stack overflows. Divide and conquer applies the same idea: split into independent subproblems, solve them separately, then combine. Merge sort and binary search are the canonical examples.",
   t:"Every recursive call consumes stack: deep recursion can overflow even when correct. And any recursive algorithm can be written iteratively — recursion is a choice of expression, not a necessity.",
   ex:["Without the last line, the descent never ends.",
       "Divide, solve, recombine: the complete pattern."]},
108:{s:"Modelling connections, and finding the cheapest path",
   e:"A graph represents items linked together: cities and roads, routers and links, tasks and dependencies. Edges may be directed or not, and carry a weight — distance, cost, time. To find the shortest path from a point, you progressively grow a set of vertices whose distance is definitively known, each time taking the nearest untreated one. It is the algorithm behind routing and route planning.",
   t:"The path with fewest steps is not necessarily the cheapest: with weighted edges you compare sums, not hop counts. And the classic algorithm assumes positive weights.",
   ex:["The weight may be a distance, a time or a cost: the principle is unchanged.",
       "The network is a graph, and routing a path search."]},
109:{s:"What the processor does between two instructions",
   e:"Executing an instruction always breaks down the same way: fetch the instruction from memory, decode it to know what to do, read the data to operate on, then execute. How that data is designated is the addressing mode: the value may be written straight into the instruction, or the instruction may hold the address to read from, or the address of a cell that itself holds the address.",
   t:"Immediate mode is fastest but the value is frozen in the program. Indirect is most flexible — it enables pointers — at the price of one more memory access. The choice is a speed/flexibility trade-off, not a matter of style.",
   ex:["No extra memory read is needed.",
       "Two memory accesses: more flexible but slower."]},
110:{s:"Three measures that do not say the same thing",
   e:"Response time is what the interactive user feels: the delay before something appears. Turnaround time covers the whole processing, from submission to complete result, which suits batch work. Throughput measures the amount of work completed per unit of time, from the system’s point of view. Standard benchmarks allow machines to be compared on the same workload, provided that workload resembles real use.",
   t:"Improving throughput can worsen response time: batching work raises overall output but makes each individual request wait. The two goals do not always align.",
   ex:["What the user feels, even if processing continues behind.",
       "The system’s point of view, not the user’s."]},
111:{s:"How space gets fragmented, and how it is reclaimed",
   e:"By repeatedly allocating and freeing areas of different sizes, free space ends up cut into small scattered pieces: there is room in total, but none large enough in one block. That is fragmentation. Compaction gathers the occupied areas back together to restore a continuous free space. In memory, garbage collection automatically frees objects nothing refers to any more, sparing the programmer from doing it — and from forgetting to.",
   t:"Garbage collection does not remove every leak: an object still referenced by mistake will never be freed, however useless it has become. And its triggering can cause a pause, which is a problem in real-time systems.",
   ex:["The room exists, but in crumbs.",
       "The programmer no longer frees it, so they can no longer forget."]},
112:{s:"Letting several tasks share one resource",
   e:"When several tasks use the same resource, only one at a time may enter the sensitive part of the code. A semaphore counts the available permits: a task takes one to enter and returns it on exit; if none is left, it waits. With a single token you get mutual exclusion. The critical section is precisely the portion of code to be protected that way.",
   t:"Protecting too broadly hurts performance, too narrowly lets inconsistencies through. And taking several locks in different orders across tasks leads straight to the deadlock seen in databases — it is the same phenomenon.",
   ex:["One token only: one task at a time may enter.",
       "Keep it short: the longer it is, the longer the others wait."]},
113:{s:"Four ways to plan for failure instead of hoping it away",
   e:"Fail-safe design makes a failure lead to a harmless state: traffic lights flashing amber rather than green everywhere. Foolproof design makes incorrect use impossible or harmless: an oven that will not heat with the door open. Fault tolerance keeps the service running despite a failure, through redundancy. Graceful degradation drops secondary functions to preserve the essential ones.",
   t:"The four are regularly confused. The test: does the failure lead to a safe state (fail-safe), does the error come from the user (foolproof), does the service continue whole (tolerance) or reduced (graceful degradation)?",
   ex:["The user cannot make the mistake, even on purpose.",
       "You choose what to sacrifice rather than losing everything."]},
114:{s:"Keeping several copies consistent, or accepting they are not yet",
   e:"When a transaction spans several databases, it must be committed everywhere or nowhere. The two-phase protocol handles that: first ask every site whether it is ready, and only if all say yes, order the commit. Replication instead keeps copies on several servers: availability and read performance improve, but copies may momentarily lag behind the original.",
   t:"Replication is not a backup: a mistaken deletion propagates to the copies. And the two-phase protocol blocks if the coordinator dies between the phases — sites stay waiting, locks held.",
   ex:["A single negative answer cancels everything, everywhere.",
       "Reads are spread out; writes stay centralised."]},
115:{s:"Choosing the wireless link by range, rate and power",
   e:"Each wireless technology occupies a niche defined by three competing constraints: range, data rate and energy use. Wireless LAN offers good throughput over a few tens of metres. Short-range links connect nearby devices while drawing little power. Low-power wide-area networks accept a very low rate to reach several kilometres on a battery lasting years — that is what makes the internet of things possible. Fifth-generation mobile aims at high speed, low latency and device density at once.",
   t:"No technology is fast, long-range and frugal all at once: those three trade against each other. The exam question always describes the use, and the use picks the answer.",
   ex:["Rate is sacrificed, and it does not matter for a few bytes an hour.",
       "Short range, low power: exactly the need."]},
116:{s:"Finding where it jams when « the network is down »",
   e:"Diagnosis proceeds by elimination, from simplest to most complex. Sending an echo packet says whether the remote machine answers. Route tracing shows which devices are crossed and where progress stops, locating the break. The network management protocol lets a central console continuously poll devices — load, errors, interface state — and receive their alerts.",
   t:"No echo reply does not prove the machine is off: many firewalls block such packets on principle. It is a clue, not proof.",
   ex:["Answer or no answer: always the first test.",
       "The break lies just after the last device that replies."]},
117:{s:"How a web page becomes unreadable to whoever intercepts it",
   e:"Public-key cryptography is secure but slow; symmetric encryption is fast but raises the problem of delivering the key. Secure communication combines both: asymmetric cryptography is used only at the start, to agree on a session key, and the rest of the exchange is encrypted with that symmetric key. The server certificate, signed by a certification authority, additionally guarantees you are talking to the right server and not to a man in the middle.",
   t:"Encryption alone is not enough: without authenticating the server you can perfectly encrypt a conversation with an impostor. That is exactly what the man-in-the-middle attack exploits, and why the certificate is indispensable.",
   ex:["You take the safety of one and the speed of the other.",
       "Without it, encryption would protect a conversation with the attacker."]},
118:{s:"Giving each person exactly what they need, and not one permission more",
   e:"The principle of least privilege holds that an account should carry only the rights strictly required by its function: if compromised, the damage stays within that scope. In practice rights are not granted to people but to roles, to which people are then attached: reassignment becomes simple, and rights no longer accumulate across moves. Administrative accounts are used only for tasks that demand them.",
   t:"The weak point is almost never the initial grant but the removal: people remember to give rights on arrival, rarely to take them away on departure or transfer. Periodic entitlement reviews exist for that.",
   ex:["After a few years, a long-timer holds every right of every past post.",
       "Working as administrator permanently turns the smallest error into a disaster."]},
119:{s:"The specialised defences, beyond the firewall",
   e:"The web application firewall inspects the content of HTTP requests and blocks those resembling an injection or a hostile script — where a classic firewall looks only at addresses and ports. The sandbox runs a suspicious file in an isolated environment to observe its behaviour without risk to the real system. Digital watermarking embeds invisible information in an image or sound that identifies its origin, which helps against unlawful copying.",
   t:"A web application firewall does not replace fixing the code: it filters what it recognises and can be bypassed. It buys time until the patch, it does not excuse not applying it.",
   ex:["It inspects content, not merely address and port.",
       "If it is malware, it only soils a disposable environment."]},
120:{s:"Describing behaviour that depends on what already happened",
   e:"An automaton is defined by a set of states, possible inputs, and a table saying which state each input leads to from each state. It models anything whose behaviour depends on the past: a vending machine by the coins inserted, a protocol by the messages received, a lexical analyser by the characters read. The exam gives a table or diagram and asks which state is reached after a sequence of inputs.",
   t:"The safe method is to unfold the input sequence step by step, noting the current state each time, skipping nothing. Guessing the final state by eye is almost the only source of error on these questions.",
   ex:["That is exactly what distinguishes an automaton from a plain function.",
       "The automaton says whether the string belongs to the language, nothing more."]}
};
